      "kind": "annonce",
      "titre": "Introducing Zero Wire, a hub for investigating critical Internet vulnerabilities",
      "titre_media": "Zero Wire",
      "date_annonce": "2026-11-03T14:00:00.000Z",
      "categories": [
        "sécurité",
        "grandes entreprises"
      ],
      "accroche": {
        "texte": "Zero Wire turns critical CVE disclosures into short, actionable briefings on Cloudflare Radar.",
        "passages": [
          "bringing the clarity of a news wire service to critical security developments",
          "Zero Wire lives inside Cloudflare Radar under the security section"
        ]
      },
      "description": {
        "texte": "When a critical disclosure lands, the slow part is usually the assembling: comparing advisories, working out which systems run the affected software, then answering the business. Zero Wire collects that starting point in one card per issue — what happened, who should look, what to do next, and the evidence behind it.\n\nEach briefing links out to CVE records, research, exploitation evidence including CISA's catalog, and vendor guidance, so engineers can go deeper while a CISO gets the short version. A pre-compiled prompt lets your agent run a read-only assessment against your own infrastructure. Cloudflare adds context from its network where relevant, and says plainly when a control only covers traffic it can see.",
        "passages": [
          "are we affected, and what are we doing about it",
          "Each briefing brings together a plain-language explanation of the vulnerability, who should investigate it, recommended actions, and links to the evidence and vendor guidance.",
          "We also offer a pre-compiled prompt you can use with your agent to assess the impact on your infrastructure.",
          "Cloudflare contributes security research, observations from our network, and practical knowledge of the controls that can help reduce exposure."
        ]
      },
      "nouveaute": {
        "texte": "A public vulnerability intelligence feed hosted on Cloudflare Radar. It aims at critical issues instead of cataloguing every CVE, and sorts briefings between two buckets, Needs attention and Reference. Each card carries a status line and a date showing when it was last verified, plus a log of what changed. Neither status nor date is a verdict on your own environment, so read them next to the evidence.",
        "passages": [
          "a public vulnerability intelligence feed on Cloudflare Radar",
          "Rather than a catalog of every CVE, Zero Wire complements vendor advisories and vulnerability databases",
          "“Needs attention” brings together issues requiring active assessment or action",
          "The status and last-checked date help you interpret each card.",
          "Read them alongside the briefing’s evidence rather than treating them as a verdict on your own environment."
        ]
      },
      "ce_que_ca_permet": {
        "texte": "Get a first read on an issue, know which teams should investigate, and leave with concrete next steps. Severity and active exploitation are kept separate so you weigh both. Where a WAF rule helps, the briefing gives its availability and configuration requirements, and points you to the dashboard.",
        "passages": [
          "A vulnerability’s severity and evidence of active exploitation are distinct",
          "the briefing reports its availability and essential configuration requirements",
          "paid Plans and free Plans links take customers to the relevant dashboard configuration"
        ]
      },
      "pour_qui": {
        "texte": "Security leads and practitioners. The same card has to work for two readers: a CISO who wants the gist and the right questions, and an engineer who needs enough technical guidance to investigate and remediate. Cloudflare customers aren't the only audience.",
        "passages": [
          "A CISO should be able to understand the issue quickly and ask the right questions.",
          "An engineer should be able to follow the same briefing to the technical guidance needed to investigate and remediate it.",
          "The information is useful whether or not you are a Cloudflare customer."
        ]
      },
      "limites_connues": {
        "texte": "Copying the agent prompt doesn't scan or change anything on its own, and a WAF rule is a compensating control, not a replacement for patching.",
        "passages": [
          "copying the prompt does not itself scan or change systems",
          "it is not a replacement for fixing vulnerable software"
        ]
      },
      "reperes": [
        {
          "texte": "Public feed on Cloudflare Radar",
          "passages": [
            "a public vulnerability intelligence feed on Cloudflare Radar"
          ]
        },
        {
          "texte": "Needs attention vs Reference",
          "passages": [
            "“Needs attention” brings together issues requiring active assessment or action"
          ]
        },
        {
          "texte": "A prompt for your own agent",
          "passages": [
            "a prompt that can be used with your AI agent"
          ]
        }
      ],
