      "kind": "annonce",
      "titre": "Lakebase branching for CI/CD: lessons from 100 PR databases",
      "titre_media": "Lakebase",
      "date_annonce": "2026-10-31T21:25:00.000Z",
      "categories": [
        "grandes entreprises",
        "hébergement et solutions locales"
      ],
      "accroche": {
        "texte": "Lakebase branching gives every pull request its own Postgres copy, ready in seconds",
        "passages": [
          "Lakebase branching for CI/CD: lessons from 100 PR databases"
        ]
      },
      "description": {
        "texte": "According to Databricks, Lakebase branches are copy-on-write: nothing is copied at creation, and storage only grows with what each branch changes. In the Snorkel AI setup described, each open pull request gets a writable Lakebase Postgres branch cloned from a continuously synced main branch, then deleted when the PR closes or left to expire on its TTL. The same pattern is sketched for agents that need a disposable place to write.",
        "passages": [
          "Lakebase is Databricks' serverless Postgres database",
          "Branches are copy-on-write. Creating a branch is a metadata operation, so no data is copied when the branch is created",
          "Snorkel AI's CI/CD pipeline gives every open pull request its own Lakebase Postgres branch, a full read/write copy of production data"
        ]
      },
      "nouveaute": {
        "texte": "Spinning up a branch is a metadata operation, so the data is not duplicated at creation; making one off a roughly terabyte copy is about as quick as off a gigabyte one, taking seconds instead of a full restore's minutes.",
        "passages": [
          "Creating a branch is a metadata operation, so no data is copied when the branch is created",
          "Creating a branch from a ~1 TB production copy takes about as long as creating one from a 1 GB database: seconds, not the ~20 minutes a full restore took"
        ]
      },
      "ce_que_ca_permet": {
        "texte": "Each branch can run migrations, write rows and alter schemas without touching production, main, or a sibling PR. Compute follows the branch and drops to zero when idle, so untouched branches cost nothing in compute.",
        "passages": [
          "Tests can run migrations, write data, and change schemas without affecting production, main, or any other PR",
          "Compute is per branch and scales to zero. Each branch has its own autoscaling compute, which scales down to zero between CI runs"
        ]
      },
      "pour_qui": {
        "texte": "Teams that want production-like data in CI and not only PR namespaces: the post also points at agents doing SQL, migrations or data changes, which likewise need a sandbox.",
        "passages": [
          "PR namespaces aren't the only workloads that need a disposable copy of production",
          "AI agents that write SQL, run migrations, or change data also need a safe place to try things"
        ]
      },
      "limites_connues": {
        "texte": "Branches hold the data version from creation, so without a TTL their storage keeps growing as main moves on.",
        "passages": [
          "a branch that stays around keeps holding those older pages, so the storage it uses grows over time"
        ]
      },
      "reperes": [
        {
          "texte": "Under a minute to a ready branch",
          "passages": [
            "The branch is ready in under a minute"
          ]
        },
        {
          "texte": "Storage grows with each branch's changes",
          "passages": [
            "Storage grows with the changes each branch makes, not the database size"
          ]
        },
        {
          "texte": "TTL removes branches automatically",
          "passages": [
            "Branch expiration (TTL) cleans up each branch automatically when its PR is done"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": {
        "id": "feed:databricks-blog",
        "nom": "Databricks Blog — flux officiel",
        "url": "https://www.databricks.com/blog/lakebase-branching-cicd-lessons-100-pr-databases",
        "capture_le": "2026-11-05T14:14:53.833Z"
      },
